Subject: A contracted layout is broken when an object in it becomes a prototype, or a prototype enters it

What this does
  - `js_aot_mark_prototype` replaces the two assignments of `is_prototype` (shape creation that names a prototype, and
    `JS_SetPrototypeInternal`). When the flag first turns on for an object whose shape has a layout id, the layout's
    `aot_layout_broken` bit is set.
  - `js_aot_content_enter_slow` sets the bit when the object entering a contracted shape is already a prototype. The
    inline `js_aot_content_enter` calls it for every layout id, whether or not any slot promise is made, so the test
    does not depend on the claims.
  - `qjs_aot_layout_build` scans the object list, the zero-reference list and the no-trace list at every stamp of a
    layout id, and sets the bit when a prototype object already holds the stamped shape.

Why we need it
  A compiled store into an existing slot of a contracted layout's object skips the `is_prototype` test. The test sends a
  store into a prototype to the engine, which moves the cache epoch. Dropping it is correct only while no object of the
  layout is a prototype, and `q2_is_proto` reads exactly `p->is_prototype`. An object leaves that state by turning into a
  prototype (the flag is one-way), or by being a prototype that adopts the shape (shapes are hashed by prototype and
  fields), or by already being one when the layout is stamped. Each of the three sets the layout's bit, which compiled
  code tests after every call and before the first store of the layout on a path.

Functions touched
  `js_aot_mark_prototype`, `js_new_shape_nohash`, `JS_SetPrototypeInternal`, `js_aot_content_enter_slow`,
  `js_aot_layout_held_by_prototype`, `qjs_aot_layout_build`.

No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
